A navigation bar (navbar) is a set of links that allow users to navigate to different sections or pages of a website. It's typically placed in the `<header>` or `<nav>` section of the page.

**Steps to Create a Navigation Bar:**

1.  **Use a `<nav>` element:** This semantically defines the navigation section.
2.  **Use an unordered list (`<ul>`):** This provides a structure for the navigation links.
3.  **Use list items (`<li>`):** Each list item represents a navigation link.
4.  **Use anchor tags (`<a>`):** Each anchor tag creates a hyperlink to another page or section.

**Example:**

```html
<nav>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
    <li><a href="/about">About</a></li>
    <li><a href="/services">Services</a></li>
    <li><a href="/contact">Contact</a></li>
  </ul>
</nav>
```

**Styling the Navigation Bar:**

-   Use CSS to style the navigation bar, including:

    -   Removing bullet points from the list items (`list-style-type: none`).
    -   Displaying list items horizontally (`display: inline-block`).
    -   Adding padding and margins to the links.
    -   Changing the background color and text color.

**Example (CSS):**

```css
nav ul {
  list-style-type: none;
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  overflow: hidden;
  background-color: #333;
}

nav li {
  float: left;
}

nav li a {
  display: block;
  color: white;
  text-align: center;
  padding: 14px 16px;
  text-decoration: none;
}

nav li a:hover {
  background-color: #111;
}
```

**Key Takeaways:**

-   Use semantic elements (`<nav>`, `<ul>`, `<li>`, `<a>`) to create a navigation bar.
-   Use CSS to style the navigation bar and make it visually appealing and user-friendly.
